  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1038 PM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nHOT WITH SCATTERED SHOWERS AND STORMS FRIDAY:\n\nHigh pressure remains centered south of the Four Corners with\noverall hot and dry conditions continuing for one more day on\nFriday. Some remnant moisture remains trapped under the high so\nthis will result in scattered afternoon storms favoring the high\nterrain. Any flooding concerns over burn scars remains very low\nand localized as the lower levels are much drier than a few days\nago. The gradient will tighten a bit as an upper low pressure\nsystem moves into the Pacific Northwest, allowing for breezy\nafternoon winds especially across northwest Colorado. Relative\nhumidity however will remain borderline for critical fire\nweather conditions so some localized critical fire weather\nconditions are possible but overall relative humidity will begin\nto increase throughout the day as the Pacific Northwest trough\npushes inland and allows the high pressure ridge to shift\neastward. \n\nCOOLER AND WETTER THIS WEEKEND AND BEYOND: \n\nA pattern change is in store, with moisture increasing Friday\nevening and overnight into Saturday. We could see some nocturnal\nconvection but overall, conditions look to be mostly cloudy by\ndaybreak on Saturday. PWAT anomalies surge upwards towards 150\nto 180 percent of normal with the deeper moisture still over the\nGreat Basin towards Nevada and northwest Utah. Storm coverage\nlooks to increase with more widespread precipitation, however\nthe abundant cloud cover could limit more robust convective\npotential due to limited surface heating. It does look like\nnocturnal convection is possible as several shortwaves move\nthrough this flow and continues into Sunday with better forcing\nSunday due to the PacNW trough driving a cold front through the\narea Sunday afternoon. It will remain active through at least\nMonday as a robust shortwave passes through behind this trough.\nThe potential for flash flooding and heavy rain will increase\nthis weekend into early next week with this moisture push,\nespecially over burn scars and vulnerable terrain. \n\nModels begin to diverge Tuesday and beyond, decreasing\nconfidence with a slight downturn in moisture and some drier air\npotentially entering from the west by mid week. Blended model\nguidance indicates less coverage mid to late week with storms\nfavoring the high terrain although some solutions indicate\nanother moisture push late next week as another low pressure\ntrough moves into the Pacific Northwest and southwest flow over\nthe region heading into Labor Day weekend. The eastern Pacific\naround the Baja Peninsula also looks to remain active with\npotential tropical storm activity through the coming week, so \nwe'll also be tracking that to see if we can tap into any of \nthat rich tropical moisture later in the week. Overall despite\nmodel differences, it looks like we are shifting towards a\ncooler, wetter and more active pattern heading into the month \nof September. Stay tuned!",
